<h2>What happens when you upload the wrong size</h2>
<p>
  Platforms handle a mismatched aspect ratio one of two ways: crop, or letterbox. Cropping is more common and more risky — the platform's algorithm picks what to trim based on general heuristics (often centering on detected faces, if any), with no understanding of what matters in your specific image. A product shot with the product off to one side, a screenshot with important text near an edge, or a group photo with someone at the frame's border can all lose exactly the part that matters. Letterboxing (adding bars to preserve the full image) is safer for content but produces a smaller, less visually prominent result surrounded by empty space — rarely the outcome anyone actually wants for a feature post.
</p>

<h2>Design at the target size, not up to it</h2>
<p>
  Always work from an image at least as large as the target dimensions, then resize down. Enlarging a smaller image to hit a target size requires the resize algorithm to invent pixel detail that was never captured, producing a visibly softer result — the same reason a small phone photo blown up to billboard size looks blurry rather than sharp. Shrinking, by contrast, discards data you don't need and looks clean at any reasonable ratio.
</p>

<h2>Why Instagram's two formats differ so much</h2>
<p>
  Instagram Post (square, 1:1) and Instagram Story (vertical, 9:16) reflect two genuinely different viewing contexts within the same app. Posts live in a scrollable feed and a grid-view profile page, where a square format has historically balanced well against both landscape and portrait source photos without excessive cropping either way. Stories and Reels take over the entire phone screen — a vertical format fills that space completely, while a square or horizontal image would leave the top and bottom of the screen empty or force an awkward crop.
</p>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your image</h2>
